+\begin{lemma}\label{lem:quadraticVariation_brownian}
+  \uses{def:brownianQuadraticVariation, lem:martingale_brownian_sq_sub_time, lem:isCadlag_brownian_sq_sub_time, lem:brownianDeterministicTime_predictable, lem:brownianDeterministicTime_progressive, lem:brownianDeterministicTime_cadlag, lem:brownianDeterministicTime_hasLocallyIntegrableSup, lem:brownianDeterministicTime_monotone, lem:brownianDeterministicTime_bot_eq_zero, lem:predictablePart_eq_of_normalized_decomposition}
+  \lean{ProbabilityTheory.quadraticVariation_brownian}
+Let \(B\) be the canonical Brownian motion. Under the usual hypotheses used
+to define the Brownian quadratic variation, its quadratic variation is
+equal to the deterministic time process at every deterministic time: for every
+\(t\in\mathbb R_{\ge 0}\),
+\[
+  \langle B\rangle_t = t
+\]
+almost surely.  Formally, the statement is
+\[
+  \operatorname{brownianQuadraticVariation}_t
+  =_{\mathrm{a.s.}}
+  \bigl(\omega\mapsto (t:\mathbb R)\bigr).
+\]
+When the available equality notion is upgraded from fixed-time modification
+to indistinguishability for càdlàg processes, this fixed-time statement
+promotes to \(\mathbb P\)-almost sure equality of the whole paths.
+\end{lemma}
+
+\begin{proof}
+The candidate Doob--Meyer decomposition of \(B_t^2\) is
+\[
+  B_t^2 = (B_t^2-t) + t.
+\]
+By Lemma~\ref{lem:martingale_brownian_sq_sub_time}, the first summand is a
+martingale, hence a local martingale, and by
+Lemma~\ref{lem:isCadlag_brownian_sq_sub_time} it has càdlàg paths.  The second
+summand is the deterministic time process \(T\).  The preceding deterministic
+lemmas show that \(T\) is strongly predictable, strongly progressive, càdlàg,
+has locally integrable running supremum, is nondecreasing pathwise, and starts
+from zero.
+
+The squared Brownian process used by the generic quadratic-variation
+definition is \(X_t=\|B_t\|^2=B_t^2\).  The displayed identity is therefore a
+normalized Doob--Meyer decomposition of \(X\), with martingale part
+\(B_t^2-t\) and predictable part \(T_t=t\).  Applying the normalized
+predictable-part comparison theorem identifies the choice-based predictable
+part used in \(\operatorname{brownianQuadraticVariation}\) with \(T\) at the
+fixed time \(t\), almost surely.
+
+In the current Lean route this last step must use a predictable-part
+identification theorem for normalized Doob--Meyer decompositions.  Without the
+normalization \(A_0=0\), the predictable part is not unique: one can shift a
+constant between the martingale and finite-variation parts.  Thus the equality
+\(\langle B\rangle_t=t\) should not be formalized as pointwise equality of
+the raw choice-based functions.  The honest theorem is an almost-sure
+fixed-time equality, or an indistinguishability statement after a separate
+càdlàg modification upgrade.
 \end{proof}


